Classical philology studies classical languages. Classical philology principally originated from the Library of Pergamum and the Library of Alexandria around the fourth century BC, and was continued by the Ancient Greeks and then the Romans throughout the Roman and Byzantine Empire. It was eventually resumed by European scholars of the Renaissance, where it was soon joined by philologies of other European (Romance, Germanic, Slavic, etc.), Asian (Cuneiform, Arabic, Sanskrit, Chinese, etc.), African (Egyptian, etc.), and American (Mayan, etc.) languages. Indo-European studies involve the comparative philology of all Indo-European languages.

